[THOUGHT] All eight evidence domains have now been systematically addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — mucocutaneous bleeding (epistaxis, gingival bleeding, petechiae, bruising);  
2) Course — chronic/recurrent since early childhood, not acute;  
3) Associated symptoms — no fever, abdominal pain, joint swelling, hematuria, or non-blanching rash;  
4) Past history — recurrent bleeding since infancy/toddler years, no prior major illness;  
5) Medications/allergies — no known exposures;  
6) Social/exposure — no dietary concerns or consanguinity reported;  
7) Physical exam — petechiae on forearms/legs/trunk, gingival bleeding on probing, dried nasal blood; lymphadenopathy/splenomegaly not specified but absence of mention in prior report suggests negative;  
8) Labs — normal CBC/smear, normal PT/aPTT, unavailable vWF/platelet function tests, unavailable LFTs.  

The consistent picture is a 4-year-old with *lifelong, mucocutaneous-predominant bleeding* and *normal platelet count and coagulation screening* — the hallmark of **von Willebrand disease (vWD)**, particularly type 1. While confirmatory testing is unavailable, the clinical triad — (1) mucosal bleeding, (2) normal platelets and PT/aPTT, (3) chronic/recurrent onset in early childhood — makes vWD the single most likely diagnosis. No other condition fits as precisely.

[FINISH]

